WHAT THIS COVERS
----------------

The Tracker's published data, and the README that describes it:

    outputs/csv_tables/*.csv
    README.md

These are licensed to you under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
International License ("CC BY 4.0").

    Human-readable summary   https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
    Full legal code          https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode

The full legal code at that second address is the license. It is incorporated
here by reference and governs in full.

Version 4.0 is chosen deliberately over earlier versions: it grants the
European Union sui generis database right alongside copyright, which the 3.0
licenses did not. Contributors and readers of this project are in the EU.


WHAT THIS DOES NOT COVER
------------------------

Third-party material. The projects cite news articles and company statements
by URL. Those pages remain the property of their publishers and are not
redistributed here. These files hold facts, links, and notes written for this
project. No article text is copied into them.

Trademarks and names. This license grants no rights in the IndustriousAF name,
in "The Promised vs. Produced Tracker", or in any other mark, logo or
brand element of IndustriousAF. Creative Commons licenses never convey
trademark rights; this paragraph says so out loud so that no reuse can be
presented as licensed, endorsed, or approved by IndustriousAF.


HOW TO ATTRIBUTE
----------------

CC BY 4.0 requires attribution. This satisfies it:

    The Promised vs. Produced Tracker, IndustriousAF.
    CC BY 4.0. https://github.com/industriousaf/promised-vs-produced-tracker

If you are citing the data in a paper, use CITATION.cff in the repository
root. It carries the authors and the version.

If you publish a figure or a number drawn from these files, name the Tracker
in the caption or the sentence. That is the whole ask, and it is the reason
this license was chosen over a public domain dedication.


A NOTE ON WHAT IS ACTUALLY PROTECTED
------------------------------------

Under Feist v. Rural Telephone (US 1991) facts are not copyrightable, and a
thin factual compilation attracts thin protection. So in the United States
this license is closer to a stated norm and a citation request than to an
enforceable property claim over every cell. That is understood and it is
fine. The labor worth crediting is the selection, the checking, and the human
sign-off on every published project, and the norm is what carries that
credit. In the EU the sui generis database right does more work, which is the
other reason for 4.0.
